From b56e6362fa8c49dd659a28b8fa1d965b60b703e4 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Bare <git@barebrowser.org>
Date: Sat, 5 Sep 2026 17:21:49 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Let a link open in the app that handles it

Android hands http(s) URLs to whichever app has claimed the domain. Patch 0005
stopped that outright, because nothing in the browser could ask for it back. The
setting it was standing in for now exists, and with it the two things the blunt
version could not offer.

Settings gains Open links in apps, with Always, Ask every time and Never. Never
is the default, so an install that predates the setting behaves as it did
before. Ask puts one dialog in front of a navigation that was already going to
leave; answering yes hands it back to the ordinary launch path, so Android still
shows its own chooser where several apps match. Always stops the suppression and
leaves every check the browser already makes in place.

The app menu gains Open in app. That part is upstream's and was already compiled
in: OpenInAppUtils asked DeviceInfo.isDesktop(), which also wants the device to
report FEATURE_PC. A phone never does, so on a desktop Android build shipped to
phones the answer was always no. What the feature needs to know is the target,
not the hardware, so it asks that instead, and device classification is left
alone as it was in patch 0076. The entry point that also places an omnibox chip
is not used; the menu item is the whole of it.

Incognito never hands a page to another app on its own, whichever mode is
stored. Sending the address out of the browser is the one thing incognito is for
not doing quietly, and a setting chosen once for ordinary browsing is not
consent to do it here. The menu item still works there and still goes through
the leaving-incognito confirmation, so the way out exists but the user has to
take it. Upstream declines this case too in the arrangement seen so far, by a
route that depends on how the URL happens to resolve, so it is stated here
rather than relied on.

Both gates sit in ExternalNavigationDelegateImpl, on hooks the component already
provides. Package resolution, intent parsing and launching stay with the code
that was already doing them.

Verified on a Pixel 3 across the three modes, the menu item under each,
redirects, typed URLs, same-host navigation, intent:// with and without a
resolvable package, fallback URLs, tel:, and incognito. Two apps competing for
one URL was not reachable on that device and is untested on hardware.
